"The prognosis is supported by negative surgical margins, absence of extraprostatic extension, and no documented lymph node metastasis. The preoperative PSA was 5.9 ng/mL, which contributes to the risk assessment alongside a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) finding on prostatectomy. These factors suggest a relatively favorable local disease profile.\n\nCountervailing findings include the absence of seminal vesicle invasion and negative lymphovascular invasion. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison is limited as the biopsy Gleason pattern and ISUP report are missing, while the surgical specimen provided the definitive grading. Missing postoperative PSA data prevents assessment of post-operative biochemical control trends. Future prognosis revision would depend significantly on obtaining a postoperative PSA value and tracking its subsequent trajectory over time. The current information provides a basis for risk stratification but lacks longitudinal biochemical markers for refinement. The fixed estimated horizon is 84.76 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 63.57–105.95 months; its coverage has not been validated."
